headers {
  X-Actor: {{actor}}
}

docs {
  Every request inherits the X-Actor header, so the audit trail attributes the whole
  collection to one identity rather than recording "anonymous" for anything you forgot to
  set it on.

  There is no Authorization header anywhere, and that is the API rather than an omission:
  it has no authentication of its own. X-Actor is therefore self-asserted - the trail
  records a claim, not a verified identity - which the README states plainly as a
  limitation.

  ## Order on an empty database

  Requests that create something store its id, and the rest of the collection reads those
  ids. On a database with nothing in it, three requests have to run in this order before
  any other will find its subject:

    1. Books > Create a book   (stores bookId)
    2. Users > Register a reader (stores userId)
    3. Loans > Lend a copy     (stores loanId)

  The folders are ordered by subject rather than by that sequence, because the catalogue is
  what the API is organised around and reading the collection top to bottom should say so.
  Running it end to end with `bru run -r` therefore answers 404 for anything scoped until
  the three above have run - that is the collection being a reference, not a test suite.

  ## The two requests that are meant to fail

  Loans > Replay the same Idempotency-Key and Loans > Reuse a key with a different request
  exist to demonstrate the idempotency contract. The first answers 201 with
  "replayed": true and creates nothing; the second answers 409. Neither is a broken
  request.
}
